WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.380
Every spike ends with potassium leaving the neuron.

2
00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:08.680
Where does all that potassium go?

3
00:00:09.633 --> 00:00:11.533
Into the gaps between brain cells.

4
00:00:12.313 --> 00:00:13.413
Those gaps are narrow.

5
00:00:14.673 --> 00:00:16.493
Together they are only about a fifth of the tissue.

6
00:00:17.567 --> 00:00:19.867
Inside a neuron, potassium is high.

7
00:00:21.107 --> 00:00:22.107
Outside, it is low.

8
00:00:22.817 --> 00:00:25.337
A burst of firing lets some potassium out.

9
00:00:26.097 --> 00:00:28.997
Which concentration changes more, inside or outside?

10
00:00:29.897 --> 00:00:31.977
And which change matters more for the voltage?

11
00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:40.930
In proportion, the outside, by a wide margin.

12
00:00:41.830 --> 00:00:46.330
Take a simple model: the space outside is a fifth of the tissue, and the firing

13
00:00:46.330 --> 00:00:48.570
neurons fill a third of the space inside.

14
00:00:49.633 --> 00:00:55.073
Then the same potassium changes the outside concentration about one and a third

15
00:00:55.073 --> 00:00:57.333
times as much as the inside one.

16
00:00:58.317 --> 00:00:59.877
the outside starts low.

17
00:00:59.877 --> 00:01:03.437
One extra mM raises it by a third.

18
00:01:04.017 --> 00:01:08.257
Losing 0.75 mM inside changes it by about half a percent.

19
00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:14.910
Potassium's equilibrium potential, E_K, depends on the ratio of outside to inside.

20
00:01:15.830 --> 00:01:20.690
In this model, the outside change moves it about 60 times as much as the inside

21
00:01:20.690 --> 00:01:21.690
change.

22
00:01:22.133 --> 00:01:28.073
In cat cortex, strong repetitive stimulation raised outside potassium to a ceiling

23
00:01:28.073 --> 00:01:29.293
of about 10 mM.

24
00:01:30.367 --> 00:01:35.207
At that ceiling, E_K sits about 32 mV more positive than at rest.

25
00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:39.240
Does the neuron's resting potential move that far too?

26
00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:46.950
Less far, but the same way.

27
00:01:47.690 --> 00:01:50.170
In a simple model, it moves about half as far.

28
00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:55.510
A resting neuron is mostly, but not only, permeable to potassium.

29
00:01:56.750 --> 00:01:57.990
Sodium pulls on its voltage too.

30
00:01:59.033 --> 00:02:03.853
A modest rise brings the neuron closer to threshold, so it fires more easily.

31
00:02:04.793 --> 00:02:06.593
More firing releases more potassium.

32
00:02:07.717 --> 00:02:09.157
That loop can feed a seizure.

33
00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:14.240
A large, sustained rise can do the opposite.

34
00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:18.360
Sodium channels inactivate, and the neuron can stop firing.

35
00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:21.490
So here is the rule.

36
00:02:21.490 --> 00:02:24.630
Small space outside, big change outside.

37
00:02:25.733 --> 00:02:31.113
A little released potassium is a big change outside, in proportion, and a tiny one

38
00:02:31.113 --> 00:02:36.173
inside. So in models like this one, E_K's shift comes mostly from outside.

39
00:02:38.267 --> 00:02:42.767
So a neuron's resting potential depends on cells that keep clearing that space.
